Role: You are an expert Python developer.
Task: Generate a complete, robust Python script that strips comments and docstrings from Python files.
Requirements
Backend Support
 * Make libcst optional via a -b, --backend CLI argument
 * Other backend options: tree-sitter and ast
File Handling
 * Support Python files without extensions
 * Use pathlib for all filesystem traversal
 * Accept multiple files/directories as input
 * If no input is provided, recursively process files in the current directory
Preservation Rules
 * Preserve shebangs, module docstrings, # type comments, and # fmt comments (dont add any other)
 * If a docstring is the only node in a function/class body, replace it with `pass` to avoid syntax errors
Validation
 * Validate the resulting code before writing to file — it must be valid Python
Concurrency
 * Use mp.imap_unordered with a fixed pool of 8 workers(no --worker cli arg needed)
Logging
 * Log any errors using loguru
CLI Behavior (default: remove inline comments only)
Flag Behavior -c, --comments Remove all comments except shebangs, # type, and # fmt 
              -d, --docstring Remove all docstrings except module docstring 
              -a, --all Remove all comments and docstrings, even preserved ones
              -t, --type Remove type annotations
              -x equals to -a and -t ( remove all comments & docstrings & type-annotations) with this diffetence that nothing will be written to file,
                 i'm in termux & im gonna use this flag to copy stripped code to clipboard using termux-clipboard-set command (to send code to an ai agent (less tokens))
                 how to copy to clipboard
                 after producing final_code, do this
                 subprocess.run(['termux-clipboard-set',final_code])

Implementation Considerations
 * Speed / performance
 * Code clarity / maintainability
 * Memory efficiency
 * Production-ready robustness
 * Feature set (streaming, adaptive, edge cases)
Environment
 * Python version: 3.14
Output: Return the full type-annotated code
